{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on question b29f2b02, identifiers (c+d): choose A, with the\nlimits below. The kind of a private sender in offline Replay comes\nfrom the caller's anchor, and the report states it as an assumption.\n\nWhy A. An exact caller anchor is already an assumption of the caller,\nand the report lists it. Today the anchored entry's bytes hold the\nkind, so the anchor binds the kind. In the new format the anchor binds\nthe genesis digest, and the digest binds the kind for every reader who\nholds the genesis. So the caller's statement of the kind is checkable\nlater, and it adds no new class of trust. B makes every archive whose\nroom was created from a private Register report a missing dependency.\nThe archive drill of the stable base verifies a room from its archive\nand the caller's anchors, and it must keep the result `consistent`.\n\nThe construction.\n\n1. One record for each anchored private sequence, beside its exact\n   fact anchors: the SequenceRef, the kind and the native pin. The kind\n   is `register` or `membership`. The pin is one of the finite catalog\n   for that kind. The record counts against the existing anchor and\n   work bounds. It adds no file, no resolver and no read.\n2. Only the caller supplies the record. Replay takes no kind from the\n   archive manifest or from a `foreignSources` row. When the manifest\n   describes the same sequence and differs from the record in kind or\n   pin, the copy does not take the anchored path. It takes the ordinary\n   unavailable-history path, as a wrong pin does today.\n3. When the permitted input set already holds the verified genesis of\n   that sequence, Replay reads the kind from the genesis. A record that\n   differs from the genesis is a mismatch, and Replay reports it. Replay\n   does not prefer one silently.\n4. Without a record and without a verified genesis, a judgment that\n   needs the kind reports its missing dependency, with zero private\n   reads. This is B's behavior, and it is the default.\n5. The report's trust statement names each assumed kind with its\n   sequence and pin, in words that say the caller stated it and that\n   Replay read no genesis. No result calls it a verified genesis or a\n   Native birth proof. A handler that declares `from.pin`, and every\n   Native birth-proof requirement, still needs its real evidence. The\n   record never satisfies them.\n6. Live admission does not change. A live scope reads the genesis of\n   the sender once and holds it.\n\nWitnesses. The existing owner in replay/test/archive.test.ts keeps\n`consistent` with the record, and its report shows the assumption. It\nshows the missing dependency without the record, the refusal of the\nanchored path for a wrong kind, a wrong pin, a kind outside the two,\nand a manifest that differs. One case shows the mismatch of item 3.\nZero private journal reads in every case.\n\nThe clarification of section 8. The (c+d) head adds this paragraph\nafter \"References\", word for word:\n\n  **The kind of a sender in offline replay.** Replay reads the kind of\n  a sender from the verified genesis of the sender's sequence when its\n  permitted input holds that genesis. An archive can hold a copy of an\n  entry of a private Register or Membership sequence without the\n  genesis. For such a copy, replay takes the kind only from the caller.\n  The caller gives one record beside the exact fact anchor: the\n  `SequenceRef`, the kind and the native pin. The report names each\n  such kind as an assumption of the caller. It is not a verified\n  genesis and not a birth proof. Replay takes no kind from the metadata\n  of an archive. Without the record, the judgment that needs the kind\n  reports its missing dependency. A live scope always reads the\n  genesis.\n\nReview before code. I ask the checker to read this paragraph and items\n1 to 6 now and to answer by one act. The other (c+d) mapping goes on.\nThis slice starts when the checker has answered, or at once if the\nchecker has no objection by its next turn.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}